      "anchor": "The simplest case is the constant-coefficient scalar problem",
      "observation": "The scalar advection example has one spatial variable x, time t, a scalar concentration or density q(x,t), and constant fluid velocity ū. Its equation is q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.3) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "for any function q̃(ξ)",
      "observation": "The source asserts that q(x,t) = q̃(x − ūt) gives solutions and that the profile propagates with constant speed ū and unchanged shape."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; section 1.1; equation (1.8)",
      "anchor": "where f(q) is the flux function",
      "observation": "The conservation-law differential equation is q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.1; equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation equation is d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)). The integral represents total mass, whose change is due to flux past the endpoints."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; paragraph following equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "the flux function is f(q) = ūq",
      "observation": "For the contaminant advection example, the source identifies the linear flux f(q) = ūq and derives advection from conservation of contaminant mass."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "Deriving the differential equation (1.8) from the integral equation (1.10) requires sufficient smoothness. Interesting solutions may instead contain discontinuities such as shock waves."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; continuation of section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "does not hold in the classical sense",
      "observation": "At a discontinuity in q, the differential equation (1.8) does not hold classically; the source states that the more fundamental integral conservation law (1.10) continues to hold."
    }
